{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from Rephidim.</b> That is to say, on this day, which is Rosh Hodesh Sivan, ויבאו מדבר סיני ויחנו שם — on that same day; and on the second day they moved and encamped opposite the mountain, as it says: ויחן שם ישראל נגד ההר. And the reason the text used the singular form ויחן and did not say ויחנו is to teach you the merit of Israel, that they all encamped with one heart to receive the Torah; for every place where it says ויחנו, they journeyed in dissension and encamped in dissension, but here it is written ויחן. <br><b>And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted:</b> ״ויחן שם ישראל נגד ההר״ — ישראל סבא. And the matter is that since we find regarding the journeys: ״ויסעו בני ישראל מרעמסס״, and afterward ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״, so too we find regarding the encampment: ״ויחנו במדבר״, and afterward immediately ״ויחן שם ישראל״. And והיה נגד ההר means precisely opposite the mountain. And understand this. <br><b>And according to the plain sense (peshat)</b>, the meaning of נגד ההר is: to the east of the mountain. On that very day it was said to Moses: ״כה תאמר לבית יעקב ותגיד לבני ישראל״, and he mentioned the men and the women regarding those commandments in which they are both obligated, and he said to them: ״אתם ראיתם אשר עשיתי״, ועתה אם שמוע תשמעו בקולי וגו', and they answered: ״ויענו כל העם יחדו וגו'. On the third day: ״וישב משה את דברי העם אל ה׳״. On the fourth day, He said to him: ״לך אל העם וקדשתם היום ומחר״, and similarly he said regarding the fourth day and the fifth day, and they prepared themselves for the third day of sanctification, which is the sixth day of the month. And thus it is made clear to you that on the sixth of Sivan the Torah was given." "<b>And know that</b> the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is that the Torah was given on the day of Shabbat, as they interpreted in tractate Shabbat, chapter אמר ר׳ עקיבא: The Rabbis taught — On the sixth of Sivan the Ten Commandments were given to Israel; R. Yosi says, on the seventh of it. Rava said: According to all, the Torah was given to Israel on Shabbat, as it is written here (Exodus 20:8): ״זכור את יום השבת״, and it is written there (Exodus 13:3): ״זכור את היום הזה אשר יצאתם ממצרים״ — just as there, בעצומו של יום, so too here, בעצומו של יום. For they disagree on the fixedness of the new moon: R. Yosi holds that the month was fixed on the first day of the week, and the Rabbis hold that the month was fixed on the second day of the week. And even though R. Yosi's reasoning is more refined, the halakhah follows the Rabbis, that the month was fixed on the second day of the week. And since this is so: on the day of Rosh Hodesh — the second day of the creation of the world — they came to the wilderness of Sinai. On the morrow, the third day, they moved from there and encamped opposite the mountain, and the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Moses the commandments of warning, and it is written: ״ויענו כל העם יחדו וגו'. The fourth day: ״וישב משה את דברי העם אל ה׳״. The fifth day: the commandments of separation and boundary-setting, and He said to him: ״וקדשתם היום ומחר״, which is the sixth day, and they prepared themselves for the third day — which is the third of sanctification — and that is the day of Shabbat and the sixth of Sivan. And from this you have learned that the Torah was given on Shabbat. And from this our Rabbis, of blessed memory, established in the Shabbat prayer: ישמח משה במתנת חלקו — because the Torah was given on Shabbat through Moses."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "Source reads נמוקו עמו (literally 'his reasoning was refined/stricter than theirs'), left untranslated as a rabbinic idiom of legal debate."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:04.860311605Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}